ls forward plus the bijection. 8/8 killed, 0 survivors.\\n+- SCOPE: `internal/terminalbackend/conformance.go`, `conformance_test.go`, `refusal_arm_inventory_test.go` (new); `manifest_test.go` (+2 evidence-timestamp cases), `terminalbackend_test.go` (+5 wire codes); `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` (+`terminal-lifecycle-conformance` acceptance case, 75->76), `traceability.go` (projection digest), `traceability_test.go`, `cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go`, `README.md`. Section bindings untouched: still `unevidenced` with honest gaps, no clause-level claims added.\\n+- STATUS: `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 (14 packages), `-race` exit 0 (14 packages), `go vet ./...` exit 0, gofmt clean, `tracecheck` exit 0. Package coverage 89.9% statements (reviewer-measured on the rev-1 tree; 90.0% was my rounding error). New files left untracked in the working tree; no commit (board checkpoints). TASK-260830-1snnef handed to review.\\n+\\n+### TASK-260830-1snnef round-1 rework: four sampled closures, closed by derivation\\n+- FINDING (F1): The refusal-arm derivation walked FuncDecl bodies only, so a package-level `var f = func() *Error {…}` arm and an aliased constructor (`refuse := mismatchf`) each shipped reachable with a new detail while both inventory tests stayed green. Both shapes measured empty in the tree, so the 193/193 ratio held while the closure claim did not.\\n+- FIX (F1, preferred over a caveat): the derivation now scans every FuncDecl body plus every package-level var initializer (attributed to the variable name), and `rejectConstructorAliases` fatals on any constructor reference outside direct-call position. Replayed both reviewer probes copy-aside with shasum-verified restore: A1 fails the bijection (194 derived vs 193 rows), A2 fatals at the alias. Zero table change — both shapes are still empty in the tree.\\n+- FIX (F2): `TestCheckErrorAllowedRefusesEveryUnlistedCode` was a 3–5-code sample named as a complement. It now derives the complement: a full-row