{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c665","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07303-b963-7e21-81f8-bb3541dfba40"},"sequence":397,"recorded_at":1780531400000789,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"19e64aa2-5eb1-4345-85e5-fc361fc0d256","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"19e64aa2-5eb1-4345-85e5-fc361fc0d256","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"19e64aa2-5eb1-4345-85e5-fc361fc0d256"},"call_id":"call_01a0730580ce7192bb3072ad2c6b68f5","text":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-22-1\",\n  \"command\": \"git diff 57afcc6 -- LOGBOOK.md | head -n 220\",\n  \"description\": \"Show this story LOGBOOK additions\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"diff --git a/LOGBOOK.md b/LOGBOOK.md\\nindex b8e9501..dbd5748 100644\\n--- a/LOGBOOK.md\\n+++ b/LOGBOOK.md\\n@@ -3,6 +3,123 @@\\n > Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.\\n > Newest entries first. One block per insight.\\n \\n+## 2026-09-05\\n+\\n+### 0935 — Provider conformance-harness leaf round-6 rework: switch vocabularies, ExecRunner frame loss, flake bound\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: TASK-260830-32jeti round-6 verdict (changes requested, two blocking findings F1/F2 plus the nondeterministic suite). Carries the reviewer's findings into the logbook — the reviewer deliberately left LOGBOOK.md out of their delta. Uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n+- FIX (F1, blocking): the three switch-shaped closed vocabularies the census could not see (envelope member set `checkResponseMembers` ← `DecodeResponse:416`, execution profile `ProfileMapping` ← `DecodeSpawnPlan:100`, transaction state `validStatusState` ← `DecodeStatusOutcome:126`) are package-level maps now (`responseMembers`, `profileNames`, `statusStates`), each consulted by a membership check, so the existing census derives them with no scanner change. Each carries a pinned-document derivation (`TestResponseMembersAreDerivedFromSpec` from the two 7.2 envelope sentences with `ok = true/false` normalized, `TestProfileNamesAreDerivedFromSpec` from the two 7.7 profile sentences with exactly-one-span guards, `TestStatusStatesAreDerivedFromSpec` from the 7.5 ProviderTransactionStatus state span), a census registration (19 → 22 vocabularies, both directions), and a production entry-point refusal row (envelope/`bogus` via DecodeResponse, `ProfileMapping(codex, bogus)`, bogus status state via DecodeStatusOutcome). `TestAllProductionSwitchesAreClassified` pins the residue: every production switch must be a classified dispatch (the one remaining switch dispatches on an already-validated state), so a new switch-shaped gate fails until classified. The \\\"closes the class by construction\\\" header now states the derived shapes plus this guard instead of an unstated-empty residue.\\n+- FIX (F2, blocking): `ExecRunner` discarded judgeable results through two races on `Host.Call`'s primary path. (a) `return Result{}, writeErr` before stdout was ever considered: an EPIPE racing the plugin's exit threw away valid correlated frames. (b) Found during this rework by driving `Run` directly (2 of 20 crash-script iters returned `read |0: file already closed`): `Wait` closes `StdoutPipe`/`StderrPipe` read ends after seeing the process exit, racing a drain still in flight — this, not EPIPE, is what misclassified the stdin-draining `exit 3` fixture. Fix per the file's own comments: judge whenever possible (non-empty stdout governs; empty stdout with a known exit classifies the crash through the Result), surface write/close/wait failures only with empty stdout and no known exit, and drain over `os.Pipe` pairs owned by `Run` so no later Close can interrupt a drain. New tests `TestExecRunnerKeepsValidFrameWhenPluginIgnoresStdin` (200 calls, zero discards) and `TestExecRunnerClassifiesCrashExitDeterministically` (50 crash calls, all `plugin exited without a response`): both FAIL on the pre-fix runner (2/200 frames discarded; crash misclassified as `runner reported no result`, each verified by swapping the pre-fix `runner.go` back with sha256-verified restore) and pass post-fix.\\n+- FLAKE BOUND: post-fix `go test ./internal/provhost/ -count=1` 30/30 green in two 15-run chunks (pre-fix: 3 red in 50 per the reviewer). The two F2 loops above are the deterministic pins; the old single-shot `TestExecRunnerReportsCrashExit` stays as a third witness.\\n+- EVIDENCE: `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `GOOS=windows go vet ./internal/provhost/` exit 0; gofmt clean; `go test ./... -count=1` green (15 packages); provhost `-race` green; provhost cover 86.5% → 86.0% (new judge/pipe branches). Widening mutants re-planted against the new maps (envelope/profile/status `\\\"bogus\\\"`, plus an unclassified-switch plant): each dies three ways — derivation, census subtest, entry-point row — with cp-aside/sha256 restores; the one vacuous-green run in the sequence (a gofmt-realigned anchor that planted nothing) was caught by the grep-plant assertion and re-run with the corrected anchor.\\n+- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n+\\n+### 0926 — Provider conformance-harness leaf round-6 rework: the vocabulary class closed by census\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: TASK-260830-32jeti round-5 verdict (changes requested, single blocking finding F1r). Test-only rework: new `internal/provhost/closed_vocabulary_census_test.go`, `requireVocabulary` comparison fix in `closed_vocabularies_test.go`. Production untouched; uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n+- FIX (F1r): the nine membership-checked vocabularies with no derivation (manifest/probe/capability/identity/identify/quiesce/spawn/status member sets plus the yolo mapping) are now derived from the pinned document — example-JSON keys for 7.3/7.4, first-sentence code spans for the capability-value sentence, TableRowAt Field rows for the 5.5 identity table, per-span member declarations for the 7.5 SpawnPlan/SafeBoundaryProof/ProviderTransactionStatus rows, braced-cell parsing for the identify-session success body, per-row provider-plus-first-flag for the 7.7 table. The census parses production source for package-level []string/map[string]bool/map[string]string with membership use (unknownMember argument, map index, range operand) and requires each of the 19 found vocabularies to carry an executing derivation, in both directions.\\n+- EVIDENCE: probe_widen.json replay, full-package runs, cp+sha256 restores: W1–W9 KILLED (each red three ways — derivation, census subtest, entry-point refusal row), W10/R2/R4 killed, R1/R3 survive as the stated behaviour-preserving bounds. Fail-closed attacks all red: wrong type name, bad cell marker, off-table identity window, unregistered planted vocabulary (`has no registered spec derivation`), blinded string-map scan (`names no production vocabulary`), dropped registration. `requireVocabulary` now uses reflect.DeepEqual, so the V4 joined-member mutant is killed by the derivation alone. Gates: build/vet/GOOS=windows vet exit 0, gofmt clean, `go test ./...` 15/15 green, provhost -race green, provhost cover 86.5% unchanged. CORRECTION (round-6 review): the \\\"15/15 green\\\" was one observation of a nondeterministic suite, not a property — the clean tree failed `go test ./internal/provhost/` 3 times in 50 runs under load (F2 below). Green-suite claims now carry a run count.\\n+- ANOMALY: the capability-value sentence shares its window with the warnings sentence, so a naive span read derives five members including warnings; the derivation truncates to the declaring sentence. The bare 5.5 closed sentence repeats in four sections, so the identity quote carries its schema opener to stay unique. The *Required order lists are excluded from the census by construction (missingMember-only use), which is what keeps R1/R3 surviving.\\n+- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n+\\n+### 0830 — Provider conformance-harness leaf round-5 rework: the 28 real survivors, closed by derivation\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: TASK-260830-32jeti round-4 verdict (changes requested, 106 mutants, 76 killed, 30 survived, 2 stated bounds, 28 real). Test-only rework in `internal/provhost` plus one comment restatement; production untouched. Uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n+- FIX (F1, blocking): the eight closed vocabularies the three existing specdoc derivations did not cover are now derived the same way in `internal/provhost/closed_vocabularies_test.go` — probe statuses and evidence from the Section 7.4 sentences, architectures from 7.4 prose cross-checked against the 7.5 probe request row, probe platforms from that row (sorted both sides: the row lists macos first, the implementation holds sorted order), quiesce blockers from the 7.5 enum sentence, identity kinds from the 5.5 table row, identify confidence and matched-evidence from the 7.5 identify-session row. Each widening mutant from the verdict battery (partial, assumed, 386, freebsd, other, legacy_alias, guess, guessed) is killed twice: once by the derivation equality, once by an exact-member refusal row through the production entry point.\\n+- FIX (F2): last-element substitution rows for both manifest order loops, first-element-unknown platform row (aix, still sorted-unique so it reaches the vocab check), and first/last missing-registry-key probe rows.\\n+- FIX (F3): NUL-injection fixtures for both SpawnPlan members that reach exec — argv element and env-literal value — via JSON `\\\\u0000` escapes.\\n+- FIX (F4): identity provider-correlation now pinned with the equal-length claude/gemini pair (both 6), with a length-equality guard so the fixture cannot go blind silently.\\n+- FIX (F5): all ten bound+1 rows plus exactness acceptances on both sides (including the first SP8 fixture on either side: 65537 refused, 65536 accepted). LESSON: a bound+1 mutant is killed only by bound+1 exactly — the first SP8 draft totalled 69,632 bytes, above even the widened bound, and survived. Same class as the token-floor finding: the obligation must carry the literal.\\n+- FIX (F6): aliasing tests for both copy guarantees (`MutationOperations`, `Capabilities`); the quiesce safe-rule comment restated to name backgroundNull as folded into !background by `rawNullableBool` (unreachable, not untested); the vacuous `runner.spawned() != 0` assertion on a Runner never handed to `RequireCapability` removed and restated as structural.\\n+- BONUS: the manifest digit-first provider-id row also kills MA8, which the verdict battery listed as killed but is now pinned at the manifest layer too.\\n+- ANOMALY: the first NUL-row draft wrote raw NUL bytes into the Go source (binary file, invalid-JSON refusal instead of the NUL arm); replaced with `\\\\u0000` escapes and verified zero NUL bytes remain. S1's ExecRunner fork/exec flake reappeared in 2 of ~30 full-package mutant runs; every affected mutant also failed its targeted test, so no verdict rests on it.\\n+- EVIDENCE: 29-mutant replay (the 28 real survivors plus MA8) with the reviewer's own harness and spec files: 29 killed, 0 survived. `go build`, `go vet`, `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, gofmt clean, `go test ./... -count=1` 15/15 green, provhost `-race` green, provhost cover 86.4% → 86.5%.\\n+- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n+\\n+### 0510 — Provider conformance-harness leaf: operation decoders, derived complements, inventory census 60 → 162\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: TASK-260830-32jeti (final leaf of STORY-260830-3jqsx1). New `internal/provhost` operation layer: manifest (7.3), probe + capability gate (7.4/8.3), profile mapping (7.7), quiescence proof (7.6), SpawnPlan for launch/resume, Provider Identity Record + identify result (5.5), and the single `(operation, operation_id)` key (7.5), with Host.Call conformance sequences (manifest/probe/resume/identify/quiesce end to end, 21 fail-closed capability-by-status tuples with zero processes started, byte-identical lost-prepare retry, changed-body `idempotency_mismatch` passthrough, child-code passthrough table). Uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n+- MECHANISM (leaf-2 item closed here): the derived refusal-arm inventory parses production AST — every `&frameFault` literal, every literal `integrity(...)` argument (with the `decodeStrictObject`-fault wrapper expanded over the derived frame detail set), every `parseMajor` rejection branch keyed by enclosing condition, and every literal first argument to the six refusal constructors — and checks both directions (every derived arm witnessed, every witness derived) plus a census floor. This leaf raised the floor 60 → 162 and added 102 witnesses, one per new arm.\\n+- BLIND SHAPES (stated, not fixed): a one-line wrapper fronting a constructor and an aliased constructor at an unexercised site are both invisible to the arm walk and the site audit; both measured empty in this tree and neither shape was introduced. The file header's \\\"non-literal shapes never silent\\\" line was corrected: constructor non-literals are fault conduits by design, not obligations.\\n+- CENSUS-FLOOR REASONING: the floor catches a silently truncated scan (empty, single-file, or short derivation fails even when every surviving arm is witnessed); the reverse witness check catches a deleted or narrowed production branch by orphaning its witness. Raising is routine with new refusals; lowering requires saying why.\\n+- DEFECT (caught by the new tests, fixed before review): the first `DecodeQuiesceProof` draft omitted both open counts from the safe-rule disjunction — `safe:true` with `open_child_count:1` validated. The per-conjunct safe-lie fixtures (one violated fact each) caught it; counts now return through `checkQuiesceCounts` into the rule.\\n+- COMPLEMENTS, NOT SAMPLES: 6 providers × 2 profiles swept; 4 statuses × 2 enabled swept for the probe rule, the pure gate, and one capability key, plus a per-key sweep over all 7 registry keys; keyed operations derived per-row from the Section 7.5 table (capture, materialize, commit, rollback, fork) with the 10 unkeyed operations refused in the same test; registries (operations, capabilities, platforms) re-derived from the Section 7.3 example.\\n+- EVIDENCE: 26 narrowing mutants (order-skip-first, bound+1, grammar weakening, gate `&&`→`||`, disjunct drops, key widening, registry deletion), each presence-asserted, full-package run, cp-aside restore byte-verified, tree hash equal before/after: 24 killed on first run, 2 survived (order checks skipping index 0 — no fixture distinguished the first element), both killed after adding first-element-substitution fixtures. Final: 26/26, 0 survivors. `go test ./...` exit 0, `-race` on provhost exit 0, `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, gofmt clean, `tracecheck` exit 0 unchanged (17/403, 74 cases — no ownership bindings added or claimed). provhost cover 86.4% (was 88.1%: the new decoders add the same constructor-error propagation branches the audit already records as the uncovered remainder).\\n+- BOUNDS (unchanged and restated): `provhost` still has no importers and imports no new package; no top-level `cmd/`; the ten leaf-2 survivors stay as stated (S11/S12/S13/U2 included — no test implies detached-descendant knowledge); S1's one-off ExecRunner fork/exec red stands recorded. New bounds: mutation receipts live in the transaction document/plugin (host holds no cache — cross-crash durability is the document's property); Section 8 cell values are evidence labels (host pins the fail-closed direction per tuple, not per cell); Section 8.2 store exclusions, commit/rollback result vocabularies, and remaining Section 7.5 request bodies are still opaque; literal secrecy and shell-string argv rules are structural, not content-admitted; record_id is shape-checked, not recomputed.\\n+- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n+\\n+### 0435 — Provider JSONL host leaf round-2 rework: arm identity, exact bounds, production-site pins\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: TASK-260830-qcosxq round-1 verdict (changes requested, 44/66 killed, 18 real survivors). Test-only rework in `internal/provhost` plus README/LOGBOOK honesty items; production untouched. Uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n+- FIX (F25, structural, first): every refusal row now names its arm — `requireFrameRefusal` (code + `member` detail + rule detail + non-retryable) for frame refusals, `requireLocalRefusal` (code + rule detail + non-retryable) for invalid_config/integrity/timeout/process arms, plus `observed`-version assertions on both mismatch paths and `status_state` on unknown. Zero code-only refusal rows remain outside the two helpers, the bound-child surfacing check, and mismatch checks paired with identity assertions. Matches the sibling leaf's 0/29 → all-asserted shape.\\n+- FIX (F21): five foreign-major malformed versions (`3.0`, `3.0.0.0`, `.0.0`, `3..0`, `3.b.c`) asserting `provider_protocol_error`; each of the four deleted `parseMajor` arms now promotes its shape to `incompatible_protocol` and reddens.\\n+- FIX (F22): token floor pinned two-sided — 31 decoded bytes refused, 32 accepted. Floors `< 8`, `< 16` killed by the 31-byte row; `< 48` killed by the 32-byte row and the 38-byte valid fixture.\\n+- FIX (F23): `readCapped` cap path tested both halves — over-cap cut to exactly cap bytes with prefix content (kills truncation removal), error-past-the-cap never reached (kills limiter removal), under/exact-cap passthrough.\\n+- FIX (F24): request side gains the exact-boundary test the response side had — limit+1 refused with arm identity, limit accepted. Halving the request bound reddens.\\n+- FIX (F26): `exit 3` / `exit 0` silent plugins driven through production `ExecRunner` + `Host.Call`; the both-sites-cleared exit-code mutant reproduces the reviewer's exact `process_failed` → `protocol_error` flip and is killed.\\n+- FIX (F27): stdin capture through production `ExecRunner` asserts frame + one `\\\\n`; the omitted-terminator mutant reddens.\\n+- FIX (F28/F29): orphaned discovery paragraph moved back above the JSONL section; the \\\"proven by narrowing mutants\\\" sentence restated to what the tests pin (arm identities, 31/32 token bytes, both exact frame bounds).\\n+- FINDING: the crash-exit test as first written was flaky — a plugin that exits without draining stdin races stdin writes into EPIPE, surfacing `runner reported no result` instead of the exit-code path. Fixed by draining stdin in the fixture scripts; the flake is in the fixture, not production.\\n+- EQUIVALENT (declared, not chased): M09b — narrowing the `checkResponseMembers` required list to drop `\\\"ok\\\"` changes nothing observable, because the missing-`ok` pre-check in `DecodeResponse` fires first on every input missing `ok`. The list entry is unreachable defense in depth. Single-site R17 (clearing only the `ExitError`-branch reassignment) is likewise a no-op because the `Result` literal already carries `ProcessState.ExitCode()`; the behavior-bearing mutant clears both sites and is killed.\\n+- EVIDENCE: 19-mutant verify sweep (`/tmp/rev2-verify.sh`, cp-aside/cp-back per mutant, presence asserted, restore byte-verified): 18 killed, 1 declared equivalent (M09b). True two-site R17 verified killed separately with the reviewer's exact flip. `go test ./...` exit 0 (15/15), `-race` on provhost exit 0, `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, gofmt gate clean, `go build` on linux/windows exit 0, `tracecheck` exit 0 unchanged (17/403, 74 cases). provhost cover 86.1% → 88.1%.\\n+- BOUND (stated plainly, widened the wrong way): `provhost` does not import `internal/provider` (only a comment at `runner.go:44`), so this leaf is not the first production caller; `provhost` itself has no production caller and there is no `cmd/ax`. Two orphaned packages, every `Host.Call` assertion made from a test. The non-retryability sentence in `doc.go` is now asserted on every refusal row rather than standing on prose.\\n+- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n+\\n+### 0429 — Provider JSONL host leaf: protocol transport, three inherited findings, one repeated harness failure\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: TASK-260830-qcosxq (story leaf 2). New `internal/provhost` (§7.2 framing/lifecycle, §7.5 dispatch names, status recovery; bodies opaque except status rules), plus F18/F19/F20 closures in `internal/provider` and `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. Uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n+- FINDING: F18 closed with the verdict's four-line pin (`ownerAttester` pointer-equals `fileOwnerUID`); SEAM1 verified red before restore. F19 closed by planting a distinct-ID candidate in each absolute dir of `TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir`; the `return out` mutant returns 1 candidate and reddens. F20 closed with a `GOOS=windows go vet ./...` CI step; a planted type error in `os_windows_test.go` verified the step sees the file.\\n+- FINDING: the §7.3/§7.5 operation registry counts 15, not 16 — the manifest JSON and the §7.5 table both list manifest, probe, launch, identify-session, quiesce, native-store-plan, capture, materialize, materialize-status, materialize-commit, materialize-rollback, resume, fork, stop, doctor. The ownership gap prose saying \\\"16-entry\\\" miscounts; the registry test derives the 15 from the pinned table in order.\\n+- ANOMALY: repeated entry 2113 exactly — the mutant sweep restored via `git checkout` on untracked files, silently no-opping, so mutants accumulated and every verdict after the first was taken on a red mask. Caught because later mutants stopped compiling. Recovery: byte-level reversal of every applied pair (one 2x over-replace repaired by hand), then a clean re-run with `cp` backups, per-mutant presence assertions, and restore verification.\\n+- DECISION: transport lives in `internal/provhost`, not `internal/provider` — leaf 1's `TestDiscoveryReachesNoProcess` fails any `os/exec` import in the discovery package, and weakening that scan would resurrect a round-3 kill. Local failures are `*axerror.Error` via six instrumented constructors (audited bidirectionally with a closed six-code set); child failures surface through `DecodeBound` under the static provider-major-2 binding and `LocalFromUntrusted` for first-frame faults.\\n+- ANOMALY: the sleep-past-deadline test exposed a real defect, not a test artifact — killing only the direct child leaves grandchildren holding the pipes, hanging the wait past the deadline. Fixed with process-group kill on unix (direct-child kill on Windows, stated) plus `WaitDelay` and stream abandonment; the test bounds elapsed time below the drain delay to pin it.\\n+- BOUND: provhost cover 86.1% statements; the remainder is constructor-error propagation branches (constructors proven total by an explicit test) and `ExecRunner` stdin-failure races. Full body vocabularies and mutation idempotency receipts belong to the conformance leaf; no §7.2 clause newly discharged (`tracecheck` unchanged at 17/403, 74 cases).\\n+- EVIDENCE: 25 narrowing mutants, each verified present, compiling, and restored — 25 killed, 0 survivors. `go test ./...` exit 0 (15/15), `-race` exit 0 in batches over all 15, `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0, `gofmt` clean, `tracecheck` exit 0, catalog generate current.\\n+- STATUS: Ready for review, handed off uncommitted.\\n+\\n+### 0327 — The fourth enumeration failure: the refusal inventory could derive nothing and pass\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: `internal/provider` round-3 rework of TASK-260830-2890sd (findings F11–F17).\\n+- FINDING: `auditRefusalInventory` derived its domain from package source, then asserted only that every derived site was exercised. Four mutants stayed green: empty domain, 1-of-N domain, skipping `provider.go` entirely, and a planted stray `Error{}` plus an empty-but-successful wrong-cwd read. The same stray alone reddened, which locates the hole precisely in the derivation, not the check. This is entry 0230's defect one level up, and the fourth recorded instance of \\\"closed the guards the review named, did not enumerate the rest\\\" — the package already carried the floor three times (`TestDiscoveryReachesNoProcess`, `TestCandidatesAdvertiseNoCapability`, the source inventory's exact counts) while the identical file-scan idiom in the refusal inventory had none.\\n+- FIX: bidirectional inventory — `ScannedFiles` floor (\\\"scanned no production sources; the check is blind\\\"), empty-sites floor (\\\"derived no refusal sites; the scan is blind\\\"), and a reverse check requiring every exercised site to resolve to a derived site. The reverse bound is derived from the test run, not hand-listed, so truncation reddens without a magic number. Floors that must survive early-return mutants live at the caller (`TestSection71AdvertisesNoPublicSDK` for `exportedSymbols`); a floor inside the helper is bypassed by `return nil` at its top. `auditRefusalInventory` floors live in the audit itself, so they hold for the same reason.\\n+- FINDING: the production `OSSystem` seam had zero negative tests — eight survivors in one file. Three are entry 0230's uid-0 twin on the attestation side (`Inspect` swallowing the error as uid 0, `fileOwnerUID` returning `0, nil` against its own doc comment, `CurrentOperatorPolicy` seeding `{0}`), one inverts the headline invariant at the only violable point (`ReadDir` error as absence). The `Inspect` attestation-error branch is unreachable with real stat results on unix, so it is forced through a documented `ownerAttester` variable — the only seam that makes the branch testable rather than merely readable.\\n+- BOUND: `Geteuid`-vs-`Getuid` is environment-equivalent on any host where euid == uid (here `euid=502 uid=502`, probed); the test pins `OperatorUID` to the effective uid and kills the `{0}`-wildcard mutant, and the residual is recorded, not waved through. Native Windows refusal is covered by a build-tagged test compiled via `GOOS=windows go vet`, not executed here.\\n+- EVIDENCE: 19-mutant harness over every rev4 survivor shape: 18 RED, 1 environment-equivalent green (G40b). `go test ./...` exit 0, provider cover 97.0%, `go vet`/`GOOS=windows go vet`/`GOOS=windows go build` clean.\\n+- STATUS: Resolved. TASK-260830-2890sd round-3 rework, handed to review uncommitted per the CR-shape instruction.\\n+\\n+## 2026-09-04\\n+\\n+### 0230 — The guard the round did not sweep was the one that matters: uid 0 admitted, suite green\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: `internal/provider` round-2 review of TASK-260830-2890sd at `d8fc669`; §7.1. 32 mutants, 30 caught, 2 survived.\\n+- FINDING: injecting `if uid == 0 { return true }` into `OwnerPolicy.Approves` (`provider.go:143`) leaves `go test ./... -count=1` GREEN across all 14 packages. No test in the package uses uid 0 — `fakeUID = 1000`, `foreignUID = 2000`, `adminUID = 7`. The property is asserted three times and measured zero times: the production doc comment at `provider.go:139` (\\\"no superuser exception is implied\\\"), the outcome artifact (\\\"no implied superuser\\\"), and the §7.1 MUST pinned verbatim at `enumeration_test.go:135` (\\\"owned by the operator or an administrator-approved identity\\\"). Root-owned system PATH directories are the first case a real host meets, so this is the realistic identity, not an exotic one. Confirmed by isolation: a probe driving `Discover` with a uid-0 file under an operator-only policy PASSes against intact production and FAILs under the injection.\\n+- ROOT CAUSE: entry 1632's generalisation, for the third recorded time. Round 2 closed the four guards the review named and did not enumerate the rest of the path. `OwnerPolicy.Approves` is the next guard inside `trustCandidate` after the two that were fixed, and `TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir` is the single-entry twin of the multi-entry PATH fixture the same round wrote — narrowing the `plugin_dirs` absolute gate to `index == 0` also survives the full suite. The remedy for \\\"proven at one sample\\\" is the traversal, not the named fix.\\n+- NOTE: the rev1 verdict supplied a complete logbook entry with an instruction to land it verbatim, and `LOGBOOK.md` is untouched in `292fc6d..d8fc669`. The lesson that would have prevented this round's omission was written into a board resource and never reached the file the next round reads. A verdict-supplied entry is part of the rework scope, not an appendix to it.\\n+- FINDING: two round-2 fixes closed the quoted mutant and not its class. The new 3x4 trust-gate table enumerates its sources from a hand-written literal and reports `12/12` as `len(sources) * len(dimensions)` compared against itself, so adding a fourth discovery source to `Discover` with every trust gate bypassed leaves `go test ./...` GREEN across all 14 packages. The package already owns the right pattern one file away — `refusal_inventory_test.go` derives refusal sites from the AST and reddens naming `provider.go:451` when an unexercised site is injected — and the table should derive its source labels from the `collectDirectory` call sites the same way. Separately, `TestVerifyDetectsLateByteDigestChange` moves the LAST digest byte, so `sum[:1]`, `sum[:16]`, `sum[:31]` and `sum[8:24]` all redden while `sum[1:]` SURVIVES: the guard moved from proven-at-byte-0 to proven-at-byte-31, the same 1-of-32 shape mirrored. A 32-subtest sweep over byte index costs microseconds and reports the ratio the domain actually has.\\n+- NOTE: the round-2 fixes are real and were each re-confirmed by re-injecting the round-1 mutant at `d8fc669` — the 12-cell source x dimension table, the PATH absolute gate on both the fake and production `OSSystem` seams, the shape-only `Verify` fixture with per-branch `Detail()` pins, the late-byte digest fixture (kills both `sum[:1]` and `sum[:31]`), and the approved-administrator owner-identity subtest. Production behaviour is correct as written; only the evidence is short.\\n+- STATUS: Pending. TASK-260830-2890sd routed to `to-dev`; no production behaviour change required.\\n+\\n+### 0210 — The inventory gate proves a site was reached, not that a test can tell which site fired\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: `internal/provider` review of TASK-260830-2890sd at `292fc6d`; §7.1. Five mutants survived the full suite.\\n+- ROOT CAUSE: every `Verify` branch reports the same `integrity_failure` code, and every subtest asserts on `Code()` alone. `TestVerifyDetectsSubstitution/replaced_with_directory` (`trust_test.go:136`) sets `fakeFile{content: nil, ..., regular: false}` — two facts at once — so deleting the non-regular check at `provider.go:455-457` leaves the whole suite green. Isolated: with the fixture changed to `content: []byte(\\\"bytes\\\")` the subtest PASSes against intact production and FAILs with the check deleted.\\n+- FINDING: this is entry 0115's own defect class, reintroduced one subtest away in the same function within the same commit that recorded fixing it. The refusal-inventory gate cannot see it. The gate requires each constructor site to be REACHED, and this fixture does reach `provider.go:456`; it cannot tell that a different site would have produced the same observable. By construction it also cannot see a DELETED site. Within one code class, \\\"site reached\\\" is not \\\"branch distinguished\\\" — the gate needs the test to assert which fact changed, and no test in the package asserts `Detail()`.\\n+- FINDING: the trust gate is proven at 1 of 3 discovery sources. Injecting `if source == \\\"path\\\" { info.IsRegular = true; info.UID = owner.OperatorUID }` into `trustCandidate` survives, as does the same for every `source != \\\"plugin_dirs[0]\\\"`. All four refusal tests use a single `/plugins` directory; PATH is exercised only positively and for duplicates. Entry 1632's rule — enumerate every guard on the path and measure each over its own domain — applies per SOURCE here, not only per guard.\\n+- FINDING: `Discover` refuses a relative `providers.plugin_dirs` entry (`provider.go:334`) but never checks `system.PathDirs()` (`provider.go:348`), and `OSSystem.Canonicalize` resolves relative entries against the process working directory via `filepath.Abs`. Probed through production `OSSystem`: `PATH=\\\".\\\"` admits an operator-owned `ax-provider-evil` planted in the CWD; `plugin_dirs=[\\\".\\\"]` refuses it. Two sources sharing `collectDirectory` do not share the gate. The owner check does not help — files in a checkout the operator owns are operator-owned.\\n+- FINDING: the digest guard is proven at 1 of 32 bytes; narrowing `provider.go:466` to `sum[:1]` survives, because both fixture pairs happen to differ in their first byte. The `identity != record.owner` half of `provider.go:458` has no test at all — `adminUID = 7` is declared at `fake_test.go:118` and never used, and it is exactly the fixture that case needs.\\n+- NOTE: §7.1 has no ownership binding at all — the registry binds 7.2/7.3/7.4/7.5/7.A/7.8/7.9 to the placeholder `catalog.ForRelease` and never mentions 7.1. The deferral reason is accurate (`traceability.go:422` pins the projection to `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`), but the section is now implemented and must be claimed before the Story closes.\\n+- STATUS: Pending. TASK-260830-2890sd routed to `to-dev`; 27 of 32 mutants caught, production behaviour correct as written.\\n+\\n+### 0115 — Provider discovery leaf: the inventory gate caught a test proving nothing, and a trust test caught a double hash\\n+\\n+- SCOPE: new `internal/provider` (Discover/Trust/Verify over a filesystem seam), `README.md` section, TASK-260830-2890sd §7.1.\\n+- FINDING: first full run was green except the new refusal-inventory gate, which named `provider.go:463` — the Verify re-read failure path. The `unreadable target` subtest set file content to nil without scripting a read error, so it exercised the digest-mismatch path while claiming the re-read path. Same class as entry 1447: a test passing while proving nothing about the path it names. Scripted `contentErr` fixed it; gate green.\\n+- FINDING: `TestTrustRecordsCandidateFacts` caught a real double hash before commit — production called `sha256.Sum256` then `scalar.SHA256Digest` (which hashes again). Single `SHA256Digest(content)` now; Verify compares one hash against the receipt in constant time.\\n+- DECISION: `ax-provider-<id>` names with an invalid suffix (e.g. `.bak`, uppercase) fail discovery with `invalid_config` rather than being skipped — a prefixed name signals intent and silent skipping would turn a typo'd install into a confusing absence. Bare non-prefixed entries are still ignored. Same-file-twice (two dirs, identical bytes) still fails: the section's MUST is unconditional and order confers no precedence.\\n+- BOUND: native Windows owner attestation is unimplemented (`os_windows.go` refuses), so external executables are undiscoverable there; builtins unaffected. Package mutates no durable state — the host persists `TrustRecord`; no crash/idempotency surface inside. Ownership JSON untouched (hash-pinned; another scope's review owns it).\\n+- EVIDENCE: `go test ./internal/provider/ -count=1` exit 0 (34 top-level PASS), cover 93.4%, `go vet`/`go build` clean, `GOOS=windows go build` clean, full `go test ./...` exit 0, tracecheck exit 0 unchanged at 17/403.\\n+- STATUS: Resolved. TASK-260830-2890sd, handed to review.\\n+\\n ## 2026-09-03\\n \\n ### 1632 — The generalisation was written down one round earlier and still not executed: a list is a forced traversal, prose is a memory\\n@@ -673,3 +790,23 @@ assertion here rather than a brittle one.\\n - NOTE: General property, applies to every derived gate — an inventory must assert that the set of constructs it scans is complete for the package, and a mutation sweep must assert its baseline is green. A gate that cannot fail when someone adds a second copy of a check is decoration, and a mutant that \\\"kills\\\" an already-red mask proves nothing.\\n - SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `README.md`\\n - STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 2.\\n+\\n+### 2321 — A local copy of a shared gate needs a pin, not a comment; a registry that only counts itself cannot see new code\\n+- ROOT CAUSE (B3): `provhost.decodeStrictObject` re-implemented strict decoding without the shared `canonicaljson` surrogate rule, and Go's `encoding/json` replaces a lone `\\\\ud800` escape with U+FFFD silently — so Section 1.6 wire bodies carrying lone surrogates were admitted. Same class as the sibling story's decoder gap.\\n+- FIX: local `hasLoneSurrogateEscape` gate in `internal/provhost/surrogate.go` (local copy because `canonicaljson` is frozen across both unlanded stories), called first from `decodeStrictObject`; one `\\\\ud800` fixture per production entry plus `TestSurrogateGateAgreesWithCanonicalJSON`, a 13-vector pin driving both the local gate and `canonicaljson.Canonicalize` so the copies cannot drift.\\n+- ANOMALY: the first gate version used `switch` and reddened `TestAllProductionSwitchesAreClassified` — the package census bans switches as membership-gate hiding spots. Rewritten with if/else, identical verdicts, green. A gate's own residue rules apply to the fix too.\\n+- ROOT CAUSE (B4): `tracecheck` verifies registry -> code only, so ~3,700 lines of `internal/provider` + `internal/provhost` landed with the gate green under gap prose asserting \\\"no provider manifest is implemented anywhere\\\". The census enumerates what registered itself.\\n+- FIX: 4 acceptance cases + 4 section bindings (7.1, 7.6, 7.7, 8), 4 gap rewrites (5.5, 7.2, 7.3, 7.5), re-pinned hash; report now bindings=53 unevidenced=45 clauses 17/428. Reverse scan (every `internal/*` package owned or explicitly excluded) recorded as open work, not implemented here.\\n+- DECISION (A2/A3/A4, recorded in code): CheckIdentity duplication is deliberate (different error surface, shared package frozen); Provider Protocol 3.0.0 is a stated non-owner bound, not a defect; provider's cause-appending errors must be rebuilt, not quoted, at the future cmd/ax lift (`refuseCausalLeak`).\\n+- SCOPE: `internal/provhost/surrogate*.go`, `protocol.go`, refusal inventory (floor 162->164), `profile_test.go` (cross-plane set test), `identity.go`/`doc.go` bounds, `internal/provider/doc.go` composition contract, ownership registry + pin + report/golden/README figures.\\n+- STATUS: Resolved. `go test ./...` and `-cover` green, vet + GOOS=windows vet + gofmt clean, admit-all and narrowing mutants red as required. TASK-260830-32jeti round-4, handed to review.\\n+\\n+### 1244 — A deadline borrowed from pipe ownership is not a deadline; a shared hand list is not a pin\\n+- ROOT CAUSE (F1): the `os.Pipe` rewrite in `internal/provhost/runner.go` fixed the Wait-closes-StdoutPipe race but removed the only thing that unblocked the post-Wait drain. `exec.WaitDelay` closes pipes exec itself owns; with caller-supplied `*os.File` pipes nothing closes them, so `readCapped` waits for EOF until every writer is gone and a setsid-detached grandchild holds `Host.Call` open for its whole lifetime (measured 20.3-20.7s against sleep 20, frame accepted with err=nil).\\n+- FIX: the waiter owns the bound now — `command.Wait()` runs in a goroutine under `select` on the call context, both drains collect through `collectDrains` under the same context, and closing the read ends fails blocked drains at once. A `Process.Kill` backstop covers a self-detached direct child, which the group-aimed `Cancel` misses. The round-6 EPIPE rule is untouched: with the wait reaped and stdout holding a frame, the frame still governs.\\n+- ANOMALY: the first regression probe passed vacuously in 0.79s — python3 cold start under load can exceed the 400ms deadline kill, so no grandchild ever existed to hold the pipe. A hang test whose fixture loses a startup race proves nothing. Rebuilt on perl (millisecond start) with the kill seconds away; 3/3 red at the sleeper lifetime before the fix, green at the deadline after.\\n+- ROOT CAUSE (F2): the 13-vector corpus was literal-versus-literal — both implementations matched the same hand list, so a narrowed low-surrogate ceiling (SG1, 1023 admitted) and a narrowed uppercase hex range (SG5, 473 admitted) survived 6/6. Raw WTF-8 (`\\\\xed\\\\xa0\\\\x80`) arrived through an encoding the escape scanner never sees and became U+FFFD silently.\\n+- FIX: `decodeStrictObject` refuses non-UTF-8 first (mirroring `canonicaljson.decodeStrict` order), and the corpus is replaced by a 239,630-vector derived sweep — full BMP x hex case, surrogate x 16 case mixes, high x boundary/non-low seconds, sampled positions plus all-surrogate member names, full BMP raw (WTF-8 form for surrogates), malformed pa\\n[tool output bounded: strategy=head, original_bytes=40678, visible_bytes=40000]\\n<system-reminder>\\nFull output saved to: /Users/iv/.local/share/muse/sessions/2026/09/05/01a07303-b963-7e21-81f8-bb3541dfba40/tool-outputs/1a515582-2347-4e9a-baa4-6eb053af97d8/call_01a0730580ce7192bb3072ad2c6b68f5-bash.txt\\nFull output is longer than the visible window.\\nUse bash tools like head, tail, cat, sed, or grep to inspect and search the full output.\\n</system-reminder>\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 40678,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 10170,\n  \"truncated\": true\n}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"bash","outcome":"success"}}}
